---
// Lesson 1.2a — The Unity Editor Tour
// The five panes, GameObject/Component/Transform, play mode vs edit mode.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.2a — The Unity Editor Tour</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Before you write a line of code you need to read the tool that runs
    everything. Unity is five windows, one Inspector, and a trap called
    play mode. Ten minutes of tour saves ten hours of confusion.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tour</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Open the Overworld scene. Find and name all five panes:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Pane</th><th>What it shows</th><th>Your move</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hierarchy</strong> (left)</td><td>Every GameObject in this scene, as a tree</td><td>Click things. Some are parents with children — why?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Scene</strong> (center)</td><td>The world, editable from above</td><td>Scroll to zoom, right-drag to pan, F to focus</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Game</strong></td><td>What the camera sees when you press Play</td><td>Switch back and forth with the tabs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Inspector</strong> (right)</td><td>Everything about the selected object — as components</td><td>Select the Player and read the list</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Project</strong> (bottom)</td><td>The repo's actual files: Scenes, Scripts, Prefabs, Artwork</td><td>Navigate to Assets/Scripts and find PlayerController.cs</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>The one idea to remember: GameObject → Components.</strong>
        Select the Player in the Hierarchy and read its Inspector: a
        <strong>Transform</strong> (position/rotation/scale), a
        <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> (physics), <strong>BoxCollider2D</strong>s
        (hitboxes), a <strong>Sprite Renderer</strong> (what you see), and a
        <strong>Player Controller (Script)</strong>. A GameObject is an empty
        container; components are the abilities bolted onto it. C# scripts
        are just one more kind of component.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Take the player apart</h3>
        <p>In the Overworld scene, select the Player and try each of these — run the game after every change and undo with Ctrl+Z:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Uncheck the <strong>Sprite Renderer</strong>. What still works, and what does that tell you about which component does what?</li>
            <li>Uncheck the Player Controller script. Does the player still fall on spikes — and why or why not?</li>
            <li>Drag the player somewhere else in the Scene view without playing. What numbers changed in the Transform?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Trap</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Play mode is a dream, edit mode is reality.</strong> Press
        Play. While the game runs, drag a Coin prefab into the scene.
        Stop the game. The coin is <em>gone</em>. Everything you change in
        play mode — positions, Inspector values, even new objects —
        evaporates when you press stop. Students lose whole afternoons to
        this every year. You will lose five minutes once, and never again.
    </div>

    <p><strong>See it happen:</strong> press Play, set the Player's
    xSpeed to 20 in the Inspector, enjoy the speedrun. Press stop. Press
    play again. What speed is it now, and where did your change go?</p>

    <p>
        The safe version of that workflow: change values in play mode to
        <em>tune</em>, then stop and enter the winning numbers in edit
        mode so they persist. That is exactly what the serialized fields
        are for — tuning knobs that live on the object, not in the code.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Why would Unity be designed so that play-mode changes vanish?
            (Hint: think about testing an idea fifty times a day, and
            what "reset to the last known good game" means.) Is this
            closer to a save-file system or an undo button, and why?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Ship It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        One more piece of housekeeping: scenes are files like anything
        else. When you move something in the Overworld, the .unity file is
        dirty — Ctrl+S saves the scene (look for the asterisk in the tab).
        Save, then commit and push: <code>"1.2a: editor tour"</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name all five panes and what each is for</li>
            <li>Explain GameObject + components with the Player as the example</li>
            <li>Demonstrate play-mode vanishing with your own two hands</li>
            <li>Save a scene and commit + push the change</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor, scripts, input, vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.2a" title="1.2a The Unity Editor Tour — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
